1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,920
Chapter 5. The American R. The tongue doesn't touch anywhere. Growl out the R in

2
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:13,100
the throat. American English today, although continually changing, is made up

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,680
of the sounds of the various people who have come to settle here from many

4
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:21,660
countries. All of them have put in their linguistic two-sense, the end result being

5
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:26,060
that the easiest way to pronounce things has almost always been adopted as the most

6
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:32,780
American. R is an exception, along with L and the sounds of a and th, and is one of

7
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:37,180
the most troublesome sounds for people to acquire. Not only is it difficult for

8
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:41,720
adults learning the language, but also for American children who pronounce it like a

9
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:46,160
w or skip over it altogether and only pick it up after they've learned all the other

10
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:53,180
sounds. The invisible R. The trouble is, you can't see an R from the outside. With

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:58,020
a P, for instance, you can see when people put their lips together and pop out a

12
00:00:58,020 --> 00:01:04,480
little puff. With R, however, everything takes place behind almost closed lips back

13
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:09,120
down in the throat, and who can tell what the tongue is doing? It's really hard to

14
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:12,960
tell what's going on if, when someone speaks, you can only hear the R sound,

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,300
especially if you're used to making an R by touching your tongue to the ridge

16
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:21,400
behind your teeth. So what should your tongue be doing? This technique can help

17
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:27,380
you visualize the correct tongue movements in pronouncing the R. 1. Hold your hand

18
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:31,920
out flat with the palm up, slightly dropping the back end of it. That's

19
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:37,000
basically the position your tongue is in when you say ah. So your flat hand will

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,920
represent this sound. 2. Now to go from ah to the er, take your fingers and curl them

21
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:50,380
into a tight fist. Again, your tongue should follow that action. The sides of

22
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:54,540
your tongue should come up a bit too. When the air passes over that hollow in the

23
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:59,920
middle of your tongue, that's what creates the er sound. Try it using both your hand

24
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:05,640
and tongue simultaneously. Say ah with your throat opened and your hand flat.

25
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:11,360
Then curl your tongue up and your fingers and say er. The tip of the tongue should

26
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:16,420
be aimed at a middle position in the mouth but never touching and your throat should

27
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:24,060
relax and expand. R, like L, has a slight schwa in it. This is what pulls the er

28
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:29,960
down so far back in your throat. Another way to get to er is to put a spoon on your

29
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:35,660
tongue and go from the e sound and slide your tongue straight back like a

30
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:39,820
collapsing accordion, letting the two sides of your tongue touch the insides of

31
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:45,160
your molars. The tip of the tongue, however, again, should not touch anything.

32
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:51,180
Now, from E, pull your tongue back toward the center of your throat and pull the

33
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:57,280
sound down into your throat, eeeerrrr. Since the R is produced in the throat,

34
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,540
let's link it with other throat sounds.
